---
/**
 * The rate card, or the honest absence of one.
 *
 * `hub.rates` is unset until there are numbers worth standing behind. Until
 * then, the page makes one concrete founding offer, the network sponsor slot
 * (`hub.sponsors.network`), with its terms but without a price: the owner
 * has not set one (5 October 2026), and a provisional price or a table with
 * blanks in it would be worse than asking.
 *
 * Audience figures are the same bet in reverse, in three states rather than
 * two: nothing counted, counting but too new to mean anything, and enough
 * weeks to quote. The middle state exists because switching analytics on
 * does not hand you an audience, and a page that starts advertising one the
 * same afternoon is making a claim it cannot support. Quoting a number
 * nobody counted is how a media kit stops being believed, and this whole
 * model runs on being believed.
 */
import Page from '@/layouts/Page.astro';
import CtaBand from '@/components/CtaBand.astro';
import { emailEditions, getHub, liveTowns, neighborsOf } from '@/config';
import { addDays, formatDate, parseLocal, startOfDay } from '@/lib/dates';

/**
 * Thirty days before the page will talk about an audience at all. A week of
 * traffic is weather, not a number a buyer can plan against, and the first
 * days after launch are mostly us reloading our own pages.
 */
const MIN_DAYS = 30;

const hub = getHub();
const live = liveTowns();
const rates = hub.rates ?? [];
// The start date is part of what makes this claim true, not decoration: with
// a counter but no date, the page falls back to saying nothing is measured
// yet, which is the one wording that cannot be wrong. The validator errors on
// that combination so it does not survive a build unnoticed.
const since = hub.analyticsSince ? parseLocal(hub.analyticsSince) : null;
const counting = (Boolean(hub.ga4Id) || hub.analytics === true) && since !== null;
// The page says when counting began, a fixed date, never how long ago: an
// age is only true on the day the page was built ("20 days ago" was stale
// by the morning after). Figures are quoted from a fixed day too, MIN_DAYS
// after the start, compared as Denver midnights.
const measured = counting && since !== null && startOfDay().getTime() >= addDays(since, MIN_DAYS).getTime();
const mailto = `mailto:${hub.social.email}?subject=${encodeURIComponent('Advertising enquiry')}`;
const foundingMailto = `mailto:${hub.social.email}?subject=${encodeURIComponent('Founding network sponsor')}`;
// The community side of "Partner with us": a chamber, a library, a
// recreation district or a volunteer group working with a guide, which is
// not a placement and costs nothing. The page had only the paid side
// (audit, 22 October 2026). Nothing here is a promise of software: every
// line is something the guides already do by email and by hand.
const communityMailto = `mailto:${hub.social.email}?subject=${encodeURIComponent('Community partnership')}`;
const sendDay = hub.newsletter?.sendDay ?? 'Thursday';
const money = (n: number) => `$${n.toLocaleString('en-US')}`;
// Editions covering more than one guide sell their email slot as one.
const shared = emailEditions().filter((e) => e.shared);
/**
 * Where the "Nearby this weekend" block can appear at all: a guide with a
 * live neighbour. The block is built from the neighbours' one-off listings
 * for the coming weekend and is not drawn when there are none, so the offer
 * says which guides and on what condition rather than "all of them" (audit
 * of 13 October 2026, F03: promised on 22, present on 19).
 */
const withNearby = live.filter((t) => neighborsOf(t).length > 0);
const withoutNearby = live.filter((t) => neighborsOf(t).length === 0);
const list = (names: string[]) => (names.length > 1 ? `${names.slice(0, -1).join(', ')} and ${names[names.length - 1]}` : (names[0] ?? ''));
---
<Page
  title="Advertise & partner"
  eyebrow="Work with us"
  description={`Reach residents and visitors across Colorado’s Front Range and mountain towns through the ${live.length} Inside the Towns guides.`}
  intro={`${live.length} independent guides to Colorado towns, on the Front Range and in the mountains, written for people deciding where to go this weekend, or whether to move here at all. Two ways to work with them: a labeled sponsorship, or a community partnership that costs nothing.`}
  narrow
>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The guides are written for people who live in, visit, or are about to move to towns and cities on Colorado’s
      Front Range and in its mountains, from Fort Collins in the north to Elizabeth south-east of Denver and up to
      Leadville at 10,000 feet. Placements are clearly labeled and never mixed into the editorial listings.
    </p>

    <h2>What isn’t for sale</h2>
    <p>
      A place in the listings. Nobody pays to be listed, to be ranked higher, to be called a pick or to change what a
      guide writes, and that is the reason anyone believes the listings. Sponsorship sits beside the editorial,
      labeled, never inside it. It is written into the <a href="/editorial/">editorial policy</a>, and it applies to
      partners and sponsors alike.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14" id="community">
    <h2>Chambers and community organizations</h2>
    <p>
      This is the other kind of partnership, and it is not a placement: a chamber of commerce, a library, a
      recreation district, a historical society or a volunteer group working with the guide for its town so the
      guide is right and the organization's work reaches the people it is for. Nothing in it is for sale and nothing
      in it buys a place in the listings.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Share verified events.</strong> You send the events your members and your calendar carry, from
        the organizer's own page or your own; the guide lists them with the source named and the day it was
        checked, free, under the same rules as every other listing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Send corrections, openings and closures.</strong> You tell us when a business opens, moves or
        closes, or when a listing is wrong; we check it against the business's own page and fix it, usually within
        a day or two, and log the change where readers can see it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Coordinate seasonal guides.</strong> You tell us what your town is planning for a season (a holiday
        market, a summer concert series, a parade) early enough for a guide to carry the dates and the facts; we
        write it from the organizers' own pages and keep it current.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Start small.</strong> <a href={communityMailto}>Email {hub.social.email}</a> with your town and
        one thing you would like to try first: a season, a calendar, a correction. We reply with what the guide can
        do and what we would need from you. The <a href="#pilot-example">example below</a> shows one shape that could take.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      What stays the same whoever we work with: the guide decides what it lists and how it describes it; nothing
      is ranked for a partner; a sponsor, if there is one, is labeled as one and sits beside the listings, never
      inside them; and no guide is affiliated with, endorsed by or speaking for a chamber, a town or any
      organization, which the <a href="/editorial/">editorial policy</a> says and each guide's About page repeats.
    </p>
  </div>

  {/* A worked example of a first community pilot (audit priority 10, 26
      October 2026), labeled as an example twice over, in the eyebrow and
      the first line, so it cannot be read as a partnership that exists.
      It names no organization, promises no result, and costs nothing: every
      line is something a guide already does by email and by hand. */}
  <aside id="pilot-example" class="mt-10 max-w-2xl rounded-lg bg-raised p-6 ring-1 ring-line-soft md:p-8" aria-labelledby="pilot-example-title">
    <p class="eyebrow">Example only · not an existing partnership</p>
    <h3 id="pilot-example-title" class="display-md mt-2 text-[1.5rem] leading-snug">What a small first pilot could look like</h3>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
      An illustration of the shape a first pilot could take with a chamber, a library or a volunteer group. It
      describes no real organization and no agreement, and no outcome is promised.
    </p>
    <dl class="mt-5 grid gap-x-6 gap-y-3 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed sm:grid-cols-[9.5rem_1fr]">
      <dt class="font-semibold">Length</dt>
      <dd>Eight weeks in one town: for instance, mid-November through the holidays.</dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold">The organization</dt>
      <dd>
        Sends its public events, or a link to its calendar, by email each Monday; passes on openings, moves and
        closures its members mention; and names one person who can answer a question about a date.
      </dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold">The guide</dt>
      <dd>
        Lists each event that checks out against the organizer’s own page, with the source named; checks and fixes
        each correction, logged on its corrections page; and writes one seasonal page, such as the town’s holiday
        markets, from the organizers’ own pages.
      </dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold">Cost</dt>
      <dd>None either way. No fee, no placement, no logo, no endorsement, and no say over what the guide writes.</dd>
      <dt class="font-semibold">At the end</dt>
      <dd>
        A plain count from the guide’s own records: events sent, events listed, corrections made. Then both decide to
        carry on, change it or stop. Either side can stop at any time with an email.
      </dd>
    </dl>
    <p class="mt-5 text-[0.9375rem]">
      <a href={communityMailto} class="font-semibold text-primary underline">Suggest a pilot for your town</a>
    </p>
  </aside>

  {rates.length > 0 ? (
    <section class="mt-14">
      <h2 class="display-md mb-6 text-3xl">What a placement costs</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="compare w-full text-left">
          <caption class="sr-only">Advertising rates</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Placement</th>
              <th scope="col">Where it appears</th>
              <th scope="col">Price</th>
              <th scope="col">How many</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {rates.map((rate) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row" data-label="Placement">{rate.name}</th>
                <td data-label="Where it appears">
                  {rate.placement}
                  {rate.note && <span class="mt-1 block text-sm text-ink-muted">{rate.note}</span>}
                </td>
                <td data-label="Price" class="whitespace-nowrap tabular-nums">
                  {money(rate.price)} <span class="text-ink-muted">/ {rate.period}</span>
                </td>
                <td data-label="How many">{rate.slots ? `${rate.slots} per town` : 'Ask'}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-[68ch] text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        We keep placements to a small number per town so they do not compete with each other, which is also why the
        right-hand column is a real limit rather than a sales line.
      </p>
    </section>
  ) : (
    <div class="prose mt-14">
      <h2>Founding network sponsor</h2>
      <p>
        One labeled sponsor line across the whole network, for a business that serves the corridor rather than one
        town. The line carries one sponsor at a time, for a three-month minimum, at a founding rate offered until the
        network publishes a rate card.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          “Nearby this weekend, presented by” your name, on that block on the {withNearby.length} of the {live.length} guides
          that have a neighboring guide, each weekend its neighbors have something on. The block is built from the
          neighbors’ one-off listings and is not drawn on a weekend with none, and {list(withoutNearby.map((t) => t.name))}{' '}
          {withoutNearby.length === 1 ? 'has' : 'have'} no neighboring guide yet, so no block.
        </li>
        <li>The same line on every This Weekend page, each guide’s and the network’s.</li>
        {hub.newsletter && <li>A labeled sponsor line in the {sendDay} email.</li>}
      </ul>
      <p>
        Each line sits beside the listings, labeled, never inside them, and buys nothing in them. The founding rate is
        not published yet: <a href={foundingMailto}>email {hub.social.email}</a> and ask for it.
      </p>
    </div>
  )}

  <div class="prose mt-14">
    <h2>What’s available</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A labeled “presented by” slot on a town’s events page, which lists what is on each week.</li>
      <li>A sponsor block on a town’s Moving here page, the guide’s page for people about to move there.</li>
      <li>
        Network-wide sponsorship across {live.length === 1 ? 'the live guide' : `all ${live.length} live guides`}, for
        anyone serving the whole corridor rather than one town.
      </li>
      {hub.newsletter && <li>A labeled slot in the weekly email, beside the listings and never inside them.</li>}
    </ul>
    {hub.newsletter && shared.length > 0 && (
      <p>
        Some neighboring towns share one weekly email: {shared.map((e) => e.name).join('; ')}. Each of those
        emails carries one sponsor line, so it reaches every town in it, while the events and Moving here placements
        on each town’s guide are still sold town by town.
      </p>
    )}

    <h2>Audience</h2>
    {measured ? (
      <p>
        Counting began {formatDate(since!)}. Ask, and we will send what has actually been counted for the towns you
        care about, with the dates it covers: visits to the guides and, for the weekly email, subscribers and opens.
        Small towns are small, and the figures will say so. Nothing is projected or estimated, and no number goes on
        this page until it can be dated and checked.
      </p>
    ) : counting ? (
      <p>
        Audience tracking began {formatDate(since!)}. Ask for the current verified traffic and newsletter figures and
        you will get exactly what the counter says, with that start date and the reporting period attached. The
        founding sponsorship terms reflect the network’s early stage. What you will not get is a projection: actual
        figures are never mixed with estimates, and no rate or audience claim is manufactured to close a sale.
      </p>
    ) : (
      <p>
        We are not going to quote an audience number we have not measured. Traffic reporting is being set up now, and
        the moment there are real figures they go here and to anyone who asks — per town, dated, and honest about
        which towns are small. If you would rather wait for numbers before talking, that is the right instinct, and we will
        come to you when they exist.
      </p>
    )}

    <h2>Getting in touch</h2>
    <p>
      <a href={mailto}>Email {hub.social.email}</a> and say which town or towns you care about. What suits a business
      serving the whole corridor is different from what suits one café, and knowing which you are saves us both a
      round of email.
    </p>
  </div>

  <CtaBand
    slot="after"
    eyebrow="Before you spend anything"
    title="Look at what you would be next to"
    body="The guides are the pitch. Read a town's events page and its Moving here page, and decide whether the company is one you want to keep."
    links={[
      { label: 'This weekend', href: '/this-weekend/', primary: true },
      { label: 'Moving here', href: '/moving/' },
    ]}
  />
</Page>
